cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use quadrivium's harmonized panels in research, please cite as below."
title: "Quadrivium: Reconstructive Harmonization of U.S. Higher-Education Survey Data"
type: dataset
version: "4.0.0"
license: CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/QuinnyXu/quadrivium"
authors:
  - name: "Quadrivium contributors"
abstract: >-
  An integrated database harmonizing multiple NSF/NCSES higher-education surveys
  into reproducible analytical panels, using Reconstructive Harmonization: each
  era is reconstructed on its own terms, methodological discontinuities are
  decomposed into named, quantified components (real growth, definitional change,
  population expansion, bounded residual), and both are published with
  documentation a cold reader can use without misreading the discontinuity.
  Version 4.0 contains three datasets joined into one integrated object. (a) HERD —
  NSF Higher Education Research and Development survey data (FY 1972–2024) across
  the 2010 instrument redesign: a 50-year field-level R&D expenditure panel
  (FY 1975–2024), a Q15/Q16 research-personnel panel (FY 2022–2024), and a Q4/Q5
  attribute sibling; this is the expenditure-OUT face (R&D spent, by institution /
  field / source class). (b) Federal S&E Support — a 53-year (FY 1971–FY 2023)
  institution-level panel of federal science-and-engineering obligations to
  higher-education institutions, drawn from the NSF Survey of Federal Science and
  Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions and
  carrying a native IPEDS UnitID; this is the funding-IN face (federal S&E dollars
  committed to individual higher-education institutions). (c) GSS — NSF Survey of
  Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering (FY 1972–2024),
  carrying a native IPEDS UnitID across all 53 years: three long-format panels
  (graduate-student federal support by mechanism and federal agency; enrollment by
  degree level, gender, and race; postdoctoral and non-faculty-researcher counts);
  this is the human-capital face — the federally-supported graduate students and
  postdocs that research funding trains. The value of the database is the integrated
  whole: HERD (expenditure-OUT), Federal S&E Support (funding-IN), and GSS
  (human-capital) are linked on the institution-year hub via the cross-survey
  institution-identity spine, so funding committed to an institution can be read
  against the R&D it spent and the researchers it trained. The funding-IN ↔
  expenditure-OUT join carries documented misalignments (federal-obligation
  fiscal-year basis vs. institution reporting-year basis, and institutional-grain
  differences); see the methods notes for the decomposition. Each dataset is
  validated against published NSF/NCSES ground truth (GSS reproduces published
  federal graduate-support counts for all 49 years FY1975–2023 with zero
  mismatches). GSS field names are provisional in v4.0: reconstructed by
  count-matching to published NSF totals (exact for the 91 of 131 codes active in
  2023; field_coarse is Science / Engineering / Health super-broad only; ~40
  historical-only codes left NULL pending the NCSES field-code reference).
keywords:
  - higher education
  - research and development
  - HERD
  - NSF
  - NCSES
  - data harmonization
  - reproducible research
  - survey methodology
  - Reconstructive Harmonization
  - federal research funding
  - Federal S&E Support
  - Survey of Federal Science and Engineering Support to Universities, Colleges, and Nonprofit Institutions
  - research funding
  - GSS
  - Survey of Graduate Students and Postdoctorates in Science and Engineering
  - graduate students
  - postdoctoral researchers
  - research workforce
  - human capital
identifiers:
